      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Common causes for chaotic conductivity readings in deep-sea deployments include biofouling interfering with electrode contact, changes in the local electrical shielding due to water pressure gradients, or issues with the sampling frequency/data transmission hardware. Rigorously check the sensor's calibration records and examine the power supply stability during that dive period. Periodic recalibration in a controlled lab environment is the most definitive way to rule out hardware drift.",
      "source": "target",
